data: {"id":"chatcmpl_793fa0e2-1c79-43bc-87de-72a431ef4efe","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792099472,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Referee assessmentC1 is well-defined under the literal signed-weight reading, but its status is unclear on the inspected evidence—not established as an open problem. The principal issue is a hypothesis mismatch: the classical first-order Bonferroni bound concerns nonnegative measures, whereas C1 allows arbitrary signed cell weights. This prevents direct application of that baseline; it does not, by itself, constitute a refutation of the particular product-weight assertion. Lean CommunityNo PDF was available in the conversation. A Library search did not locate the named manuscript. The assessment therefore uses the confirmed statement and supplied source excerpts, without claiming to have inspected PDF pages or authenticated the diagnostic publication.C1: Definitions, quantifiers, and hypothesesThe supplied definitions resolve the model as follows. A listing is a function\n$$g:A\\times A\\longrightarrow Y,$$\nwith column-dependent product weight\n$$w_q(g)=\\prod_{a\\in A}\\prod_{b\\in A}q\\bigl(b,g(a,b)\\bigr).$$\nIts twisted diagonal is the function\n$$d_{f,g}(b)=f(g(b,b)).$$\nA row  $a$  is captured when  $g(a,b)=d_{f,g}(b)$  for every  $b\\in A$ , and  $C_f(g)$  counts such rows. This is the matrix notation for the diagonal/off-row coordinates in the supplied Sample, listing, and sampleWeight definitions.The literal assertion quantifies universally over finite  $A,Y$ , every real-valued  $q:A\\times Y\\to\\mathbb R$ , and every  $f:Y\\to Y$ . It imposes neither nonnegativity nor column normalization. The definitions of  $T_k,B_k$  use  $k\\in\\mathbb N$ , with the usual convention  $\\binom{c}{k}=0$  for  $k\u003ec$ . Empty sums and products retain their ordinary conventions, so empty index sets do not make the statement undefined.Two presentation issues should be corrected. First, write the product explicitly:  $q$  depends on the column and the cell value, not on two arbitrary cell indices. Second, state whether\n$$\\sum_{y\\in Y}q(b,y)=1\\qquad(b\\in A)$$\nis intended. This hypothesis appears in the supplied exact-capture theorems but not in C1. It cannot silently be inherited from the phrase “source model.” Even with that normalization, signed weights should be described as signed masses, not automatically as probabilities.Inspected baseline and its limitsThe directly applicable classical comparison is the finite union bound. Its nonnegative-measure formulation is explicitly available as MeasureTheory.measure_iUnion_fintype_le in Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.Basic [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/OuterMeasure/Basic.html]. It requires neither independence of the events nor probability normalization. Lean CommunityThe binomial-moment interpretation is also standard: Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640], inspected in version 2, §1, equations (1)–(3), identifies Bonferroni intersection sums with binomial moments of the event count. Its setting is a probability space, not arbitrary signed mass. arxiv.orgFor source comparison, let  $E_a$  denote the event that row  $a$  is captured. Expanding the supplied definitions and rearranging finite sums identifies  $T_1$  with the mass of  $\\bigcup_aE_a$ , and  $B_1$  with the sum of the individual event masses. Consequently, the modified statement with nonnegative cell weights is a specialization of the finite union bound. That comparison does not establish C1 with its original hypotheses. Lean CommunityThere is a further distinction in the supplied repository material: its one-row and all-orders intersection identities permit real weights subject to column normalization. Such algebraic identities must not be conflated with an inequality requiring order information. The diagnostic hits about causal transport and zeta-function definitions do not supply a matching statement; the uniform-weight bridge concerns a restricted specialization.The inspected sources establish the classical baseline, but neither contains a matching theorem or refutation for C1’s complete signed-product domain. In particular, the probability-space assumptions in the binomial-moment reference cannot simply be dropped. arxiv.orgCorrectness, escape, information gain, and utilityCorrectness: not_checked. No proof was submitted. The confirmed issue is failure of the proposed classical-source match, not an established negative mathematical result about C1.Open/known status: unclear. Neither a matching signed-product result nor a documented matching refutation was inspected. Calling C1 open would turn an incomplete search into a mathematical status claim; calling it known_false merely because general signed measures lack the relevant positivity would also skip a necessary model-specific comparison.Proof escape: undetermined. For the nonnegative modification, substitution of the capture events into the union bound and normalization of notation would be bind_only relative to that baseline. For C1 itself, the conditional additional obligation is precisely\n$$\\sum_g\n\\left(C_f(g)-\\mathbf 1_{\\{C_f(g)\\ge1\\}}\\right)\n\\prod_{a,b\\in A}q\\bigl(b,g(a,b)\\bigr)\\ \\ge 0$$\nfor every allowed  $A,Y,q,f$ . This is an expansion of the requested conclusion, not a new result or a live witness. The inspected positive-measure theorem does not discharge it. Lean CommunityUtility: general_theorem, with status insufficient. A valid general theorem could have mathematical utility without an artificial downstream application. Here, however, the submission supplies neither an established signed-weight result nor sufficient evidence of its research status or contribution.Information gain and settlement: both null. No information-gain analysis with the required evidence is supplied, and C1 claims no named open-problem settlement.The recommendation is revise: make the domain and normalization explicit, separate the classical nonnegative specialization from the signed assertion, and provide source evidence addressing the exact latter statement. This is feedback on the submission’s evidential position, not a publication decision.JSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"C1 is mathematically well-defined under the literal interpretation allowing arbitrary signed, unnormalized real column weights. The inspected classical baseline establishes the nonnegative-measure specialization, not C1 as stated. No matching signed-product theorem or documented matching refutation was inspected, so the status is unclear rather than established open, known_true, known_false, or special_case_of_known. Proof escape and general-theorem utility remain undetermined and insufficient respectively.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The supplied model definitions identify a finite algebraic assertion with explicit weights and an event-count readout.\",\n    \"The distinction between tail mass and a binomial moment permits a precise comparison with the classical finite union bound.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"Arbitrary signed cell weights do not supply the nonnegative-measure hypothesis of the inspected union-bound baseline.\",\n    \"Column normalization appears in supplied exact-capture identities but is absent from C1; it must not be silently inherited or discarded.\",\n    \"No PDF was available in the conversation, and the named manuscript was not located by the Library searches.\",\n    \"Diagnostic retrieval metadata and source excerpts do not independently authenticate a frozen publication, prove entailment, or establish novelty.\",\n    \"No inspected source establishes the exact signed-product assertion's known or open status.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"note\": \"Well-posedness is assessed from the literal confirmed statement together with the supplied model definitions. The definite finding is a hypothesis mismatch with the classical positive-measure baseline, not a verified refutation of C1. Correctness remains not_checked because no proof is submitted. Escape is undetermined because neither a matching binding derivation nor evidence of a non-binding proof is available. General-theorem utility is insufficiently e"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_793fa0e2-1c79-43bc-87de-72a431ef4efe","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792099472,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"stablished; this does not impose an artificial application requirement. Information gain is null, and no named settlement is claimed.\",\n      \"known\": [\n        \"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.Basic [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/OuterMeasure/Basic.html]\",\n        \"Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640]\"\n      ],\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": true,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"Using the supplied source definitions, listings are all functions g:A×A→Y, their weights are w_q(g)=∏_{a∈A}∏_{b∈A}q(b,g(a,b)), and the twisted diagonal is b↦f(g(b,b)). C_f(g) is the cardinality of the set of rows a satisfying g(a,b)=f(g(b,b)) for every b. T_k and B_k are finite real sums, with k∈N and the standard binomial and empty-sum/product conventions. The literal universal domain includes empty finite sets, arbitrary signed q, and every self-map f. Neither normalization nor nonnegativity is stated. The phrase 'source model' should nevertheless be clarified because supplied source theorems sometimes assume normalized columns.\",\n        \"status\": \"unclear\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"The opened sources support a positive-measure union bound and its binomial-moment interpretation. They do not establish the exact universal inequality for the constrained product weights of C1 with arbitrary signs. No matching prior theorem or documented matching refutation was inspected. A missing positivity hypothesis blocks the classical application but is not, alone, a model-matching refutation. The bounded search also does not establish that C1 is an open research problem.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.Basic [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/OuterMeasure/Basic.html]\",\n          \"Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"undetermined\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"After expanding the definitions, the nonnegative modification is a binding specialization of the finite union bound. C1 retains arbitrary signed product weights, and the inspected baseline does not discharge its ordered conclusion. No proof is supplied from which to identify a non-binding step, and missing evidence cannot establish either bind_only status or new mathematical content.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"Display the listing space, the column-indexed product w_q(g), and the row-capture predicate explicitly.\",\n          \"State explicitly whether column normalization is absent or required; retain arbitrary signs only if that is the intended domain.\",\n          \"Separate the classical nonnegative-weight specialization from the signed-weight question and label the former as established background.\",\n          \"Provide an accessible source addressing the exact signed-product domain before assigning a known or open status; preserve all hypotheses in that comparison.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodology\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"C1 quantifies over finite A,Y, arbitrary q:A×Y→R, and f:Y→Y. It defines T_k as the sum of sample weights over C(s)≥k and B_k as the sum of binom(C(s),k) times the sample weight. No nonnegativity or normalization is stated.\",\n            \"location\": \"Provided review input, CONFIRMED STATEMENTS, entry id C1, statement; repeated in SOURCE CONTEXT. No PDF location was available.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"In the supplied model, a sample is (X,R), where X:A→Y and R_a:(A\\\\{a})→Y. Its listing satisfies g(a,a)=X(a) and g(a,b)=R_a(b) for b≠a. Its weight is (∏_b q(b,X(b)))(∏_a∏_{b≠a}q(b,R_a(b))). Capture at a means g(a,b)=f(g(b,b)) for every b, equivalently f(X(a))=X(a) and R_a(b)=f(X(b)) for every b≠a.\",\n            \"location\": \"Provided diagnostic receipt receipts[0].result.hits[0].source_excerpt, D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteProductCapture.lean, OffRow, Sample, listing, sampleWeight, Captured, targetRows, and captured_iff_twisted_diagonal. This is an input excerpt, not an independently authenticated repository source.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"The supplied all-orders identity assumes ∑_{y∈Y}q(b,y)=1 for every b. For H⊆A it expresses the mass of simultaneous capture at every a∈H as the product over b∈H of ∑_{f(y)=y}q(b,y)^{|H|}, multiplied by the product over b∉H of ∑_y q(b,y)q(b,f(y))^{|H|}. Its displayed hypotheses do not require q≥0. It is an equality under normalization, not an arbitrary-signed union inequality.\",\n            \"location\": \"Provided diagnostic receipt receipts[0].result.hits[4].source_excerpt, D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteProductSetCapture.lean, fixedPowerMass, collisionPowerMass, setCaptureProbability, and set_capture_probability_exact. This is an input excerpt, not an independently authenticated repository source.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For a finite index type and an outer measure μ valued in the extended nonnegative reals, μ(⋃_i E_i)≤∑_i μ(E_i). Independence and probability normalization are not hypotheses.\",\n            \"location\": \"Outer Measures documentation and MeasureTheory.measure_iUnion_fintype_le.\",\n            \"source\": \"Mathlib.MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure.Basic [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/OuterMeasure/Basic.html]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For finitely many events in a probability space, the sum of their k-fold intersection probabilities equals E[binom(N,k)], where N counts the occurring events.\",\n            \"location\": \"Version 2, Section 1, equations (1) and (2), with the probability-space assumptions immediately preceding them.\",\n            \"source\": \"Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Conversation Files semantic search: 'bonferroni signed weights finite independent listing model T_1 B_1 arbitrary signed real q'; the tool reported no conversation files.\",\n          \"Library search: exact title 'bonferroni-signed_weights', followed by 'Bonferroni signed weights'; the named manuscript was not located, and the broader search returned unrelated material.\",\n          \"General web search queries: '\\\"Bonferroni\\\" \\\"signed measures\\\"'; '\\\"FiniteBonferroni\\\" \\\"trureturing\\\"'; '\\\"union bound\\\" \\\"signed\\\" \\\"measure\\\"'.\",\n          \"General web search queries: 'Bonferroni inequalities binomial moments nonnegative weights'; 'Bonferroni inequality signed product measures negative weights diagonal capture'.\",\n          \"Opened arXiv:1511.06640v2 in HTML and inspected its probability-space assumptions and introductory binomial-moment formulas.\",\n          \"Opened official Mathlib OuterMeasure.Basic documentation and raw source; inspected the nonnegative codomain and finite-union theorem.\",\n          \"Opened the MIT OpenCourseWare Bonferroni resource page; its retrieved page did not add a decisive signed-product result and is not used as a baseline.\",\n          \"Attempted direct access to trureturing FiniteBonferroni.lean through raw main and DEV URLs and a GitHub DEV blob URL; these returned access errors.\",\n          \"Compared the supplied diagnostic source excerpts by their actual definitions and hypotheses. No MathSciNet or zbMATH database search, exhaustive citation search, or canonical-publication validation was performed.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"insufficient\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"Expanding the capture predicate identifies events E_a. Finite-sum recombination gives T_1 as the mass of their union and B_1 as the sum of their masses. With nonnegative cell weights, applying the positive-measure baseline and normalizing notation yields the modified inequality. That is not C1's domain. The supplied normalized signed intersection identities can be instantiated only with their normalization hypo"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_793fa0e2-1c79-43bc-87de-72a431ef4efe","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792099472,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"thesis and provide equalities, not the missing order statement. No assumption substitution or projection supplies positivity from arbitrary real q. The unrelated diagnostic hits provide no matching prerequisite.\",\n        \"delta\": \"Conditional obligation if C1 is proved: for every finite A,Y, arbitrary q:A×Y→R, and f:Y→Y, establish ∑_{g:A×A→Y}(C_f(g)-1_{C_f(g)≥1})∏_{a∈A}∏_{b∈A}q(b,g(a,b))≥0, without adding positivity, normalization, nonemptiness, or restrictions on f. This is the expanded requested inequality, not a proved lemma, proposed proof, or live witness.\",\n        \"escape\": \"undetermined\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"insufficient\",\n          \"rationale\": \"An established general result could have mathematical utility without a separate downstream application. Here the signed-weight result, its exact literature status, and any contribution beyond the inspected baseline remain unsupported by proof or matching source evidence.\",\n          \"problem\": \"Does T_1≤B_1 hold for every finite independent column-weighted listing model with arbitrary signed real cell weights?\",\n          \"progress\": \"The definitions and the classical nonnegative specialization have been identified. No result is established for the full signed assertion.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"Finite A,Y; arbitrary q:A×Y→R; f:Y→Y; the full listing space, product weight, capture count, and finite sums specified in C1. The supplied exact-capture identities additionally assume normalized columns, which C1 does not state.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"The model can be read from the provided definitions. Neither the universal signed inequality nor a matching prior resolution has been discharged. No proof or certified refutation was submitted.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"No manuscript PDF was accessible; all local locations refer to the provided review input, not inspected PDF pages.\",\n          \"The null-source diagnostic entries record supplied text only. Their provenance, hashes, freeze status, and reported compilation are not independently verified.\",\n          \"The trureturing FiniteBonferroni source outside the supplied excerpts remains not_checkable after the recorded access failures.\",\n          \"The positive-measure baseline is inspected, but the exact signed-product comparison remains insufficient.\",\n          \"No model-matching counterexample is asserted; failure of a theorem's hypothesis is not itself a refutation.\",\n          \"No proof is submitted, so correctness is not_checked, shape is null, and witnesses are empty.\",\n          \"No exhaustive novelty conclusion, computational-utility certificate, information-gain measurement, Lean verification, or AURIC/FIB measurement is claimed.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limits\": [\n    \"The report concerns the single confirmed assertion C1 and the explicitly recorded search scope.\",\n    \"The inaccessible PDF prevents checking manuscript wording, page locations, and any hypotheses not included in the review input.\",\n    \"Status unclear denotes insufficient exact-status evidence; it does not endorse the assertion as a valuable open conjecture.\",\n    \"The established hypothesis mismatch, conditional proof-escape obligation, unestablished utility, and absent information-gain evidence are separate findings.\",\n    \"Opened references are model-reported sources, not venue-verified references.\",\n    \"The proposed knowledge query below has not been executed as a subsequent system knowledge query.\",\n    \"The recommendation is feedback and does not decide publication.\"\n  ],\n  \"text\": \"The submission should distinguish an established nonnegative-weight specialization from its unverified signed-weight assertion. Make the product model and normalization explicit, and obtain a source comparison preserving the exact hypotheses before presenting C1 as known or open.\",\n  \"knowledge_queries\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"definitions\": [\n        \"Listings are all g:A×A→Y, equivalently the source diagonal/off-row Sample coordinates.\",\n        \"w_q(g)=∏_{a∈A}∏_{b∈A}q(b,g(a,b)); weights depend on column b.\",\n        \"d_f,g(b)=f(g(b,b)); C_f(g)=#{a∈A:∀b∈A,g(a,b)=d_f,g(b)}.\",\n        \"T_k=∑_{g:C_f(g)≥k}w_q(g); B_k=∑_g binom(C_f(g),k)w_q(g), k∈N.\"\n      ],\n      \"hypotheses\": [\n        \"A and Y are finite, including empty cases.\",\n        \"q:A×Y→R is arbitrary and may have either sign.\",\n        \"f:Y→Y is arbitrary.\"\n      ],\n      \"quantifiers\": \"For every finite A,Y, every q:A×Y→R, and every f:Y→Y, form the full listing space and the defined finite sums. No nonnegativity or column-normalization hypothesis is stated. Use standard empty-sum/product and binomial conventions.\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"T_1≤B_1.\",\n      \"search_terms\": [\n        \"first-order Bonferroni inequality\",\n        \"Boole union bound\",\n        \"finite signed product measure\",\n        \"column-weighted finite array\",\n        \"twisted diagonal capture\",\n        \"event multiplicity\",\n        \"binomial moments\",\n        \"weighted tail bound\",\n        \"positivity of integration\",\n        \"normalization versus nonnegativity\",\n        \"signed-weight union-bound counterexample\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Retrieve an exact theorem or documented refutation preserving the shared column weights and twisted-diagonal events. Distinguish arbitrary signed, normalized signed, and nonnegative domains; a generic signed-measure example or a probability-space theorem alone does not settle this constrained assertion.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"},"finish_reason":null}]}

